  "intro": "A vector has both magnitude (length) and direction. You can describe one geometrically as an arrow from an initial point to a terminal point, or analytically in component form $\\langle a, b \\rangle$, where $a$ is the horizontal change and $b$ is the vertical change. From two endpoints $P(x_1, y_1)$ and $Q(x_2, y_2)$, the vector is $\\langle x_2 - x_1, y_2 - y_1 \\rangle$. You add, subtract, and scale vectors component by component. The length is $\\lVert \\vec{v} \\rVert = \\sqrt{a^2 + b^2}$, the direction angle satisfies $\\tan\\theta = \\frac{b}{a}$ (placed in the correct quadrant), and a vector with magnitude $r$ and direction $\\theta$ has components $\\langle r\\cos\\theta, r\\sin\\theta \\rangle$.",
  "items": [
    {
      "type": "worked-example",
      "id": 1,
      "prompt": "A vector has initial point $P(-2, 3)$ and terminal point $Q(4, -1)$. Write the vector $\\vec{PQ}$ in component form.",
      "solution": {
        "steps": [
          "To go from $P$ to $Q$, subtract the initial point from the terminal point: $\\vec{PQ} = \\langle x_2 - x_1, \\, y_2 - y_1 \\rangle$.",
          "Horizontal change: $x_2 - x_1 = 4 - (-2) = 6$.",
          "Vertical change: $y_2 - y_1 = -1 - 3 = -4$.",
          "Assemble the components: $\\vec{PQ} = \\langle 6, -4 \\rangle$."
        ],
        "answer": "$\\langle 6, -4 \\rangle$"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "worked-example",
      "id": 2,
      "prompt": "Given $\\vec{u} = \\langle 2, -1 \\rangle$ and $\\vec{v} = \\langle -3, 4 \\rangle$, compute the linear combination $3\\vec{u} - 2\\vec{v}$.",
      "solution": {
        "steps": [
          "Scale each vector by distributing the scalar to both components.",
          "$3\\vec{u} = 3\\langle 2, -1 \\rangle = \\langle 6, -3 \\rangle$.",
          "$2\\vec{v} = 2\\langle -3, 4 \\rangle = \\langle -6, 8 \\rangle$.",
          "Subtract component-wise: $3\\vec{u} - 2\\vec{v} = \\langle 6 - (-6), \\, -3 - 8 \\rangle$.",
          "Simplify: $\\langle 6 + 6, \\, -11 \\rangle = \\langle 12, -11 \\rangle$."
        ],
        "answer": "$\\langle 12, -11 \\rangle$"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "worked-example",
      "id": 3,
      "prompt": "Find the magnitude of $\\vec{v} = \\langle -6, 8 \\rangle$.",
      "solution": {
        "steps": [
          "Use $\\lVert \\vec{v} \\rVert = \\sqrt{a^2 + b^2}$ with $a = -6$ and $b = 8$.",
          "Square each component: $(-6)^2 = 36$ and $8^2 = 64$. Note that squaring a negative gives a positive.",
          "Add: $36 + 64 = 100$.",
          "Take the square root: $\\sqrt{100} = 10$."
        ],
        "answer": "$10$"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "worked-example",
      "id": 4,
      "prompt": "Find the direction angle $\\theta$ (with $0^\\circ \\le \\theta < 360^\\circ$) of $\\vec{v} = \\langle -1, \\sqrt{3} \\rangle$.",
      "solution": {
        "steps": [
          "Find the reference angle from $\\tan\\theta_{\\text{ref}} = \\left| \\frac{b}{a} \\right| = \\left| \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{-1} \\right| = \\sqrt{3}$.",
          "Since $\\tan 60^\\circ = \\sqrt{3}$, the reference angle is $60^\\circ$.",
          "Determine the quadrant: $a = -1 < 0$ and $b = \\sqrt{3} > 0$, so the vector points into Quadrant II.",
          "In Quadrant II, $\\theta = 180^\\circ - \\theta_{\\text{ref}} = 180^\\circ - 60^\\circ = 120^\\circ$."
        ],
        "answer": "$120^\\circ$"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "worked-example",
      "id": 5,
      "prompt": "A vector has magnitude $8$ and direction angle $\\frac{2\\pi}{3}$. Write it in component form.",
      "solution": {
        "steps": [
          "Use $\\vec{v} = \\langle r\\cos\\theta, \\, r\\sin\\theta \\rangle$ with $r = 8$ and $\\theta = \\frac{2\\pi}{3}$.",
          "Evaluate the trig values: $\\cos\\frac{2\\pi}{3} = -\\frac{1}{2}$ and $\\sin\\frac{2\\pi}{3} = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}$.",
          "First component: $8 \\cdot \\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right) = -4$.",
          "Second component: $8 \\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} = 4\\sqrt{3}$.",
          "Assemble: $\\vec{v} = \\langle -4, 4\\sqrt{3} \\rangle$."
        ],
        "answer": "$\\langle -4, 4\\sqrt{3} \\rangle$"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "practice",
